TestNG supports data-driven tests with @DataProvider, a method returning Object[][] or an Iterator<Object[]>, where each row becomes one invocation of the test method:

```java
@DataProvider(name = "logins")
public Object[][] logins() {
    return new Object[][] {
        {"valid@example.com", "Correct1!", true},
        {"valid@example.com", "wrong", false}
    };
}

@Test(dataProvider = "logins")
public void login(String email, String password, boolean ok) {
    assertEquals(loginPage.login(email, password).isLoggedIn(), ok);
}
```

Parallelism is configured in testng.xml with the parallel attribute set to methods, classes, tests or instances and a thread-count; data providers can also run in parallel with parallel = true. With parallel execution, a shared static WebDriver breaks immediately, so I keep one driver per thread, typically in a ThreadLocal<WebDriver> created in @BeforeMethod and quit and removed in @AfterMethod. @BeforeMethod runs before each test method, while @BeforeClass runs once before the first test method of the class.

I would locate the header cells first so I find columns by name instead of by position, then scan the rows:

```java
public BigDecimal priceFor(WebDriver driver, String product) {
    WebElement table = new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(10))
        .until(ExpectedConditions.visibilityOfElementLocated(By.id("products")));
    List<String> headers = table.findElements(By.cssSelector("thead th"))
        .stream().map(WebElement::getText).map(String::trim).toList();
    int nameCol = headers.indexOf("Product");
    int priceCol = headers.indexOf("Price");
    for (WebElement row : table.findElements(By.cssSelector("tbody tr"))) {
        List<WebElement> cells = row.findElements(By.tagName("td"));
        if (cells.get(nameCol).getText().trim().equals(product)) {
            return new BigDecimal(cells.get(priceCol).getText().replaceAll("[^0-9.]", ""));
        }
    }
    throw new NoSuchElementException("Product not found: " + product);
}
```

Looking up columns by header text survives column reordering, and BigDecimal avoids floating-point rounding for money. The currency stripping is simplistic and would need care for locales that use commas as decimal separators. For pagination I would loop: search the current page, then click Next until it is disabled. For large tables, a single XPath that finds the row by text is faster than reading every cell, because each getText is a round-trip to the browser.

I would compare each adjacent pair instead of sorting a copy, because a single pass is enough:

```java
public static boolean isSortedIgnoreCase(List<String> items) {
    for (int i = 1; i < items.size(); i++) {
        if (String.CASE_INSENSITIVE_ORDER.compare(items.get(i - 1), items.get(i)) > 0) {
            return false;
        }
    }
    return true;
}
```

This runs in O(n) time and O(1) extra space, while copying and sorting would be O(n log n). Empty and single-element lists are sorted by definition and the loop handles them. For descending order I would reverse the comparison, and for prices I would parse to BigDecimal and use compareTo. I would also clarify whether the application sorts with locale-aware rules, because accented characters may need a java.text.Collator to match the application's behaviour.
